     | ALTER TABLE "sales"."invoices" NO FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY

## text
PLAN: 9 change(s), 9 suppressed  —  gate verdict REQUIRES-APPROVAL

  create-extension   citext
  update-extension   pgcrypto -> 1.3
  set-ext-schema     hstore -> sales
  replace-policy     tenant_write on sales.orders
  create-policy      new_policy on sales.invoices
  enable-rls         sales.orders
  force-rls          sales.orders
  disable-rls        sales.invoices
  no-force-rls       sales.invoices

NOT PROPOSED — differences Strata saw and will not act on:

  sales.orders                 [not-compared]
      policy owner_only matches on command, roles and permissiveness, but its USING/WITH CHECK expressions could not be rendered by the server, so they were NOT compared
  plpgsql                      [not-modelled]
      the project declares extension plpgsql in schema sales and it is installed in pg_catalog, but the extension is not relocatable, so nothing can move it
  sales.forced_only            [not-modelled]
      row-level security is FORCED here but not enabled, which has no effect.
  sales.ledger                 [not-modelled]
      row-level security is enabled and not forced, so the table's owner bypasses every policy. The project declares nothing about this table's row-level security, so the 1 policy here was NOT compared and nothing changes them: ledger_read (restrictive select for auditor, app_user)
  sales.loose                  [not-modelled]
      row-level security is DISABLED here, so the 1 policy on this table restricts NOTHING and every role sees every row: loose_read (select for app_user)
  sales.orders                 [not-modelled]
      row-level security is DISABLED here, so the 4 policies on this table restrict NOTHING and every role sees every row: tenant_read (select for APP_USER); tenant_write (update for app_user); owner_only (all commands for app_user); undeclared (delete for admin)
  sales.vault                  [not-modelled]
      row-level security is ENABLED here and there are no policies, so every row is hidden from every role except the table's owner. The project declares no policy for this table, so nothing here will change that.
  sales.orders                 [not-modelled]
      this table carries 1 policy/policies the project does not declare, and a policy is never dropped because removing one exposes rows silently: undeclared
  postgis                      [not-modelled]
      extension postgis is installed and the project does not declare it; an extension is never dropped because DROP EXTENSION takes every object it owns (version 3.4)

  [REQUIRES-APPROVAL] installs extension citext
         why:  Additive — nothing that works today stops working. But an extension brings its own types, functions and operators, and it is a lot of them: citext alone installs 88 catalog objects. None of them is managed by this project, and dropping the extension later would take all of them at once.
         next: Confirm this extension is meant to be part of the database, then approve.

  [REQUIRES-APPROVAL] updates extension pgcrypto to 1.3
         why:  An extension's upgrade script can alter or drop the extension's own objects. Strata cannot read that script, so it cannot say what this changes — only that something in the database changes that this project does not describe.
         next: Read the extension's upgrade notes for this version, then approve.

  [REQUIRES-APPROVAL] moves extension hstore to schema sales
         why:  Every unqualified reference to one of the extension's functions or types stops resolving unless the new schema is on the search path. Strata cannot find those references: they are in application SQL and in other objects' bodies.
         next: Confirm the new schema is on the search path everywhere this extension is used, then approve.

  [REQUIRES-APPROVAL] replaces policy tenant_write on sales.orders
         why:  The old policy is dropped and the new one created. If the new one admits fewer rows, queries start returning less and nothing errors; if more, rows the old one hid become visible.
         next: Compare what the two admit, then approve.

  [REQUIRES-APPROVAL] creates policy new_policy on sales.invoices
         why:  What a policy does depends on state outside itself. On a table where row-level security is off it changes nothing; once it is on, a restrictive policy can hide rows from every role. Strata cannot tell which rows it admits — that is the expression's business, at query time.
         next: Confirm which rows this admits, and for which roles, then approve.

  [REQUIRES-APPROVAL] enables row-level security on sales.orders
         why:  Every query against this table starts being filtered. With no policy admitting them, rows are hidden from every role except the owner — the table reads as empty and nothing errors. Strata cannot say which queries depend on seeing every row.
         next: Confirm a policy admits the rows each role needs, then approve.

  [REQUIRES-APPROVAL] forces row-level security on sales.orders
         why:  Policies start applying to the table's OWNER as well — usually the role that runs migrations and batch jobs. Those stop seeing rows the policies do not admit, and a write that violates a policy is rejected outright.
         next: Confirm the owner's own queries and writes satisfy the policies, then approve.

  [REQUIRES-APPROVAL] disables row-level security on sales.invoices
         why:  Every row this table's policies were hiding becomes visible to every role that can read the table. Nothing errors, and nothing in the database records that it used to be hidden.
         next: Confirm the rows here are meant to be readable by every role with access, then approve.

  [REQUIRES-APPROVAL] stops forcing row-level security on sales.invoices
         why:  The table's owner starts bypassing every policy on it, so anything running as the owner sees every row again.
         next: Confirm the owner is meant to bypass these policies, then approve.


